\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec6 1. Frontend (User Interface)\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Tool: Tkinter (Pythons built-in GUI library)\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Purpose: Displays buttons, input fields, and graphs; plays sounds.\strokec5 \
\
\strokec6 Components:\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Main Window: Buttons for each feature (e.g., \'93Play Random Frequency,\'94 \'93Input Mood\'94).\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Input Fields: Text boxes for users to enter feelings or desired emotions.\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Output Area: Shows comparisons (e.g., \'93You felt calm, 60% of users felt the same\'94).\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Graph Canvas: Displays frequency vs. emotion data\strokec5 \
\
\strokec6 2. Backend (Logic and Data)\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Language: Python\'97handles logic, file I/O, and basic data processing.\strokec5 \
\
\strokec6 Components:\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Frequency Generator: Uses PyDub or NumPy + PyGame to create and play frequencies.\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Data Storage: A simple CSV file or SQLite database to store user inputs and collective feelings (e.g., frequency, user_feeling, timestamp).\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Emotion Mapping: A dictionary or basic ML model (later) to link frequencies to emotions (e.g., 432 Hz \uc0\u8594  \'93calm\'94).\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Comparison Logic: Calculates stats (e.g., % of users feeling X at Y Hz).\strokec5 \
\
\strokec6 3. Audio Handling\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Tool: PyGame (for playback) + NumPy (to generate sine waves for frequencies).\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Purpose: Plays random or mood-based frequencies through your Mac speakers.\strokec5 \
\
\strokec6 4. Data Visualization\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Tool: Matplotlib\'97Pythons plotting library, integrates with Tkinter.\strokec5 \
\strokec6 Purpose: Graphs frequencies (x-axis) vs. emotions (y-axis, encoded as numbers or categories).\strokec5 \
